1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,535
A mango tree.

2
00:00:01,535 --> 00:00:02,932
Grass at its feet.

3
00:00:02,932 --> 00:00:05,686
Ants on the bark, a crow on a branch.

4
00:00:06,086 --> 00:00:11,085
Moss on the wall, an earthworm in wet
soil, a butterfly on a hibiscus flower.

5
00:00:11,535 --> 00:00:15,843
Diya took one slow walk through the
college garden and started counting

6
00:00:15,843 --> 00:00:17,479
kinds of living things.

7
00:00:17,828 --> 00:00:20,290
Before she reached the
gate, she had lost count.

8
00:00:20,790 --> 00:00:25,568
By the end, you will know what
biodiversity means, and why biologists must

9
00:00:25,568 --> 00:00:27,690
sort living things into groups.

10
00:00:28,340 --> 00:00:30,233
So what is biodiversity?

11
00:00:30,583 --> 00:00:31,975
Bio means life.

12
00:00:31,975 --> 00:00:33,899
Diversity means variety.

13
00:00:33,899 --> 00:00:38,690
Biodiversity is the number and
the types of organisms on Earth.

14
00:00:39,140 --> 00:00:40,980
Not how many individuals.

15
00:00:40,980 --> 00:00:45,190
A thousand ants of one kind
add only one kind to the count.

16
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:50,281
Scientists have described about one
point seven to one point eight million

17
00:00:50,281 --> 00:00:51,402
kinds so far.

18
00:00:51,402 --> 00:00:53,375
And the list keeps growing.

19
00:00:54,025 --> 00:00:58,565
Every time someone explores a new
forest, or even looks again at an old

20
00:00:58,565 --> 00:01:00,365
one, new kinds turn up.

21
00:01:01,015 --> 00:01:04,606
Let's look closer at Diya's
garden, one corner at a time.

22
00:01:04,956 --> 00:01:07,148
The mango tree alone is a home.

23
00:01:07,148 --> 00:01:11,087
Its leaves, bark, flowers and
fruit feed different visitors.

24
00:01:11,587 --> 00:01:13,063
Zoom in on the bark.

25
00:01:13,063 --> 00:01:14,339
Ants march up it.

26
00:01:14,339 --> 00:01:16,692
Moss and lichen patches cling to it.

27
00:01:17,192 --> 00:01:18,938
Zoom into the soil below.

28
00:01:18,938 --> 00:01:22,817
Earthworms, beetle grubs and
fungus threads live in the dark.

29
00:01:23,317 --> 00:01:26,950
Zoom into one drop of soil
water, under a microscope.

30
00:01:26,950 --> 00:01:30,371
Bacteria and tiny protists,
far too many to count.

31
00:01:31,021 --> 00:01:33,966
Now imagine studying
every kind one by one.

32
00:01:33,966 --> 00:01:35,643
You would never finish.

33
00:01:35,993 --> 00:01:40,743
Each place also gives the same plant
or animal a different local name.

34
00:01:40,743 --> 00:01:42,724
Names alone cause confusion.

35
00:01:43,174 --> 00:01:44,694
So biologists sort.

36
00:01:44,694 --> 00:01:49,242
Classification means grouping things
into convenient categories, based

37
00:01:49,242 --> 00:01:51,620
on features you can easily see.

38
00:01:52,270 --> 00:01:55,072
Watch a simple sort run on Diya's garden.

39
00:01:55,422 --> 00:01:56,327
Question one.

40
00:01:56,327 --> 00:01:58,891
Does it make its own food and stay rooted?

41
00:01:58,891 --> 00:02:01,215
Plants go one way, animals the other.

42
00:02:01,715 --> 00:02:03,799
Question two, for the animals.

43
00:02:03,799 --> 00:02:05,593
Does it have jointed legs?

44
00:02:05,593 --> 00:02:08,268
Ants, butterflies and beetles step aside.

45
00:02:08,768 --> 00:02:09,902
Question three.

46
00:02:09,902 --> 00:02:11,410
Three pairs of legs?

47
00:02:11,410 --> 00:02:13,020
Those are the insects.

48
00:02:13,520 --> 00:02:17,423
With three questions, a messy
garden becomes a few neat groups.

49
00:02:18,073 --> 00:02:20,179
Why does grouping help so much?

50
00:02:20,179 --> 00:02:21,643
One step at a time.

51
00:02:21,993 --> 00:02:22,893
Step one.

52
00:02:22,732 --> 00:02:27,062
Learn one member of a group, and you
already know a lot about the rest.

53
00:02:27,812 --> 00:02:28,712
Step two.

54
00:02:28,686 --> 00:02:31,916
Groups make a new organism
easier to identify.

55
00:02:31,916 --> 00:02:33,728
You know where to look.

56
00:02:34,478 --> 00:02:35,411
Step three.

57
00:02:35,411 --> 00:02:39,830
Groups give every organism a fixed
place, so scientists everywhere mean

58
00:02:39,830 --> 00:02:41,026
the same thing.

59
00:02:41,776 --> 00:02:42,676
Step four.

60
00:02:42,639 --> 00:02:47,163
Groups show which organisms are
closely related, and which are distant.

61
00:02:47,913 --> 00:02:49,976
But groups can be made in two ways.

62
00:02:50,326 --> 00:02:55,393
Early humans grouped organisms by use:
for food, for clothing, for shelter.

63
00:02:55,393 --> 00:02:57,836
Mango and rice both went into food.

64
00:02:58,286 --> 00:03:00,950
Biologists group by features of the body.

65
00:03:00,950 --> 00:03:04,437
Mango is a tree with broad
leaves, rice is a grass.

66
00:03:04,437 --> 00:03:06,445
They go into different groups.

67
00:03:07,045 --> 00:03:07,992
Your turn.

68
00:03:07,992 --> 00:03:11,115
Grouping animals into
pets and farm animals.

69
00:03:11,115 --> 00:03:13,672
Is that a scientific classification?

70
00:03:13,672 --> 00:03:14,847
Yes, or no?

71
00:03:14,847 --> 00:03:16,240
Pause and decide.

72
00:03:19,440 --> 00:03:20,340
No!

73
00:03:19,923 --> 00:03:23,730
A cow and a dog sit in one
body group, the mammals.

74
00:03:23,730 --> 00:03:28,010
Pets and farm animals is a
grouping by use, not by features.

75
00:03:28,660 --> 00:03:29,854
Remember this.

76
00:03:29,854 --> 00:03:34,696
Biodiversity counts kinds, and
classification sorts them by features.

77
00:03:35,296 --> 00:03:37,069
Now, a worked example.

78
00:03:37,069 --> 00:03:40,511
Sort five garden organisms
with the same questions.

79
00:03:40,861 --> 00:03:43,232
The mango tree makes its own food.

80
00:03:43,232 --> 00:03:44,132
Plant.

81
00:03:44,293 --> 00:03:46,579
The earthworm has no jointed legs.

82
00:03:46,579 --> 00:03:48,252
Animal, not an insect.

83
00:03:48,702 --> 00:03:51,098
The crow has no jointed legs either.

84
00:03:51,098 --> 00:03:53,590
It has two legs, wings and feathers.

85
00:03:54,040 --> 00:03:57,262
The ant has jointed legs,
three pairs of them.

86
00:03:57,262 --> 00:03:58,162
Insect.

87
00:03:58,429 --> 00:04:01,701
The butterfly also has
three pairs of jointed legs.

88
00:04:01,701 --> 00:04:04,326
Insect, in the same group as the ant.

89
00:04:04,976 --> 00:04:06,442
Now, the exam trap.

90
00:04:06,792 --> 00:04:11,584
Many students say biodiversity means
the number of living individuals.

91
00:04:11,984 --> 00:04:15,760
But biodiversity is the
number and types of organisms.

92
00:04:15,760 --> 00:04:19,222
A thousand ants of one
kind are still one kind.

93
00:04:19,872 --> 00:04:21,289
Where is this asked?

94
00:04:21,289 --> 00:04:25,929
As one line questions: the meaning of
biodiversity, and the number of species

95
00:04:25,929 --> 00:04:26,829
known.

96
00:04:27,109 --> 00:04:28,326
Exam level now.

97
00:04:28,326 --> 00:04:30,121
Read the four statements.

98
00:04:30,121 --> 00:04:31,812
Which ones are correct?

99
00:04:31,812 --> 00:04:33,081
Pause and solve.

100
00:04:38,481 --> 00:04:39,704
The answer is C.

101
00:04:39,704 --> 00:04:41,695
Statements one, two and four.

102
00:04:42,195 --> 00:04:43,518
Three is wrong.

103
00:04:43,518 --> 00:04:47,351
Biodiversity counts kinds
and types, not individuals.

104
00:04:47,851 --> 00:04:51,525
One and four come straight from
the meaning of classification.

105
00:04:51,525 --> 00:04:53,962
Two is the number of species described.

106
00:04:54,562 --> 00:04:56,024
Here is your recap.

107
00:04:56,024 --> 00:04:57,526
Screenshot this card.

108
00:05:00,726 --> 00:05:03,562
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

109
00:05:03,962 --> 00:05:04,862
One.

110
00:05:04,548 --> 00:05:07,303
What exactly does biodiversity count?

111
00:05:08,203 --> 00:05:09,103
Two.

112
00:05:08,746 --> 00:05:11,929
Why can't we study every
organism one by one?

113
00:05:12,829 --> 00:05:13,729
Three.

114
00:05:13,470 --> 00:05:17,150
Why are pets and farm animals
not a scientific group?

115
00:05:18,250 --> 00:05:21,698
Next time: sorting the
kitchen spice box is easy.

116
00:05:21,698 --> 00:05:23,659
Who sorts the living world?

117
00:05:23,659 --> 00:05:26,416
We find out in taxonomy and systematics.
